Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 09:59:39 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: QUESTION: Is writing more satisfying than conversation?

Tim May asks:
>Is the electronic discourse of this sort of list more satisfying to people
>than ordinary verbal discourse?
>* Data Point: Saturday afternoon I stopped off at Robin Hanson's house to
>pick up the "Physics and Computation" Proceedings he had so kindly gotten
>for some of us. We talked for about 30 minutes or so about the conference,
>about crypto protocols, about the information theory thread, and about a
>few other things. But, speaking again only for myself, it seemed a bit
>strained, a bit low-energy. As if we each had things to write little essays
>about, to develop theories about, but could not efficiently get them across
>in the typical American style of conversational repartee. We both even
>talked about how less information seems to be gotten across in direct
>encounters than in electronic encounters. (If true, this goes against most
>of the comments about higher bandwidth in personal encounters. Whatever is
>gained by higher bandwidth may be partly taken away by the imposed
>conversational patterns.)

Well I do feel a disappointment in personal meetings, that conversation
didn't get to all the interesting stuff that I would later remember I wanted
to talk about.  But I still have to say that per hour, personal conversation
is more valuable than the same time spent reading and posting email.  Even
Saturday's conversation with Tim.  You also have to consider that it takes
time to establish a rapore in a new medium.  It took time for this list to
get to know each other and establish a culture and style.  And it takes time
to get used to the style of taking to a new person.  I'm sure if Tim had an
office down the hall from mine and we could go to lunch frequenty, we would
soon adjust and have a great time arguing over various stuff.  At least I
would.  

>Europeans allow longer conversational blocks, more "speechifying," than
>Americans are used to. Perhaps the thrust of this post is that I'm getting
>more and more uncomfortable with the banalities of typical repartee.

As folks who know me can attest, I'm in my element with just 1-3 other
people in a "debate" style conversation - with some central "big" question
or claim at issue, and diving into great detail to sort it out.  Wandering
conversation, constantly jumping off on new tangents, leaves me cold.  I
know my style is unusual, but I really enjoy talking with the few people I
know who share it, such as Eric Drexler and Mark Miller.

Robin Hanson

