Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 12:30:53 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: MISC: Interesting Stories in the NEws

Subject: ECON: US Federal government finances distribution 

(a previous message posted:)

Within one week in early February I heard two different
pundits throw around approximately the same statistic: $119
billion a year in entitlement (social security, government
pensions, farm and other subsidies) go to individuals whose
incomes are already $50,000.  Pretty amazing to hear that one
after all these years!

(end quote)

Eder, speaking for myself:

I am interested in finding out whether the assumption that some
people may make that 'The US government generally moves money from
richer to poorer' is correct.  I have a suspicion that in reality,
the money moves from poorer to richer.

A complete analysis would look at all the government inputs -
taxes and collections of all kinds - and the distribution of
incomes from which it comes.  It would also look at all the governemnt
outputs - including govt. employee salaries, contractor salaries,
and government debt payments.

As an example, treasury bonds are held more often by richer people
than poorer people, so that part of the output tends to go to the
rich.

My question for the net community - are any of you aware of such an
analysis that has been done before?  If so, I will not undertake
the detailed analysis of the federal finances.  

The relevance to Extropians - If we show that the government actually
moves money from poorer to richer, and we propagate that fact, it
will tend to undermine the legitimacy of the current system, thus
making it easier to install a better system.

Dani Eder
 







