Tim;

The implication is that individuals can synthesize arbitrarily detailed
fictional tales when requested to, not that people could experience
precognition. There was no implication whatsoever that the "remembered"
experiences of the individuals hypnotized have any relationship to
reality -- only that, if told under hypnosis "you are a hunter in ancient 
ethiopia -- tell me about your life", you will likely generate a
story on the fly -- and one that bears no more semblance to reality than
a fiction you would devise while in a normal state.

In fact, Piaget once noted as an adult that he had memories of an event
in his early childhood that never took place -- having been told enough
times of it by his nanny, he came to believe it so deeply that he remembered
it even though it was later revealed that it had never happened. Memory
is a fickle thing. The mere fact that someone remembers something under
hypnosis does not mean it happened.

Perry


