Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 15:32:20 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: MEDIA: Extropianism considered harmful?

   From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)

   My question:  WHY are people neophobes?  WHY aren't they content
   to "live and let live"?

As a fanatical cynic, I suspect its because most people would not
benefit from rapid change, or from a lassez-faire (sp?) political
system.  Certainly, if you don't understand technology, the widespread
introduction of a new, tricky, and poorly understood technology can
only worsen your position with respect to those who do understand
technology.  Similarly, a strict free-market system disproportionately
helps those who have the skills or attitudes necessary to prosper
under a free-market system.

Just imagine yourself as a unionized GM worker with a high-school
education, who's been assembling cars for 25 years.  Now imagine:

    New technology: "We're going to install robots to assemble cars.
    You will need a college degree to be able to understand how to program
    them."

    Live and let live: "We're going to move the factory to Mexico, because
    there, wages are 1/10 of what they are here."

For most people, the most valuable thing they possess it the position
they already have in the social structure.  And the knowledge they
possess is very narrowly specialized for that situation.
Historically, things have changed very slowly, so the average human is
not equipped to efficiently exploit rapid change.  Hence, the average
human has a powerful incentive to prevent rapid change.

Dale

