	Before I became an extropian in name, I used to ask college
students "If you had a way of living forever, would you?"  My poll was
not very scientific, but I recall about half said yes and half said no.
Of those who said no, most were Christians who didn't want to miss out
on Heaven.  However, at least one was a woman from China who believed
in reincarnation.  She said that she didn't want to miss out on the
lessons of being other people in other situations, which she saw as
natural and desirable.  In other words, death served a beneficial
purpose to her.  I consider her a deathist, though I doubt she would
have supported legal intervention against immortalists.

	One of the people who said yes was the woman's fiance,
an American, who believed in reincarnation though he changed religions
at the drop of a hat.  (He was a "seeker", which in practice meant he
was a slut for religious memes.)  He seemed to believe in reincarnation
because he wanted to live forever, but had no confidence that he would
be able to.  He seemed to take comfort in reincarnation because he
hoped that the universe was on his side and that eternal life through
many lives was guaranteed.  He sounded hopeful about cryonics and
life extension, but was not willing to drop his hopes that "spiritual"
immortality was already a given.

	I'm not sure if I should classify him as a deathist.  I tend
not to.

	So, Carol, let me ask YOU the question now.  If you had a
means of living forever as yourself, would you?  In other words,
reincarnation and other such "spiritual" means are out.  I am refering
to physical immortality, through means such as cryonics, life extension,
uploading, and other extropian-correct methods.  And I do mean that you
would honestly _try_ to live forever, not fall back on reincarnation
after a few million years.

