Date: Mon, 8 Mar 93 13:38:48 MST
From: sneal@muskwa.ucs.ualberta.ca (Steve Neal)
Subject: MEDIA: Deathist Bias in Popular Culture

I, too, have been pondering some of the points that Derek Zahn raises
in his post about un-Extropian memes in popular culture.  I had planned
to make a few posts about the weather and sports before sending the
following essay to the list, but I fear someone might beat me to the punch
if I don't fire it off now.  Therefore...
 
 
                  DEATHIST BIAS IN POPULAR CULTURE
                  --------------------------------
 
                            Introduction
 
     One of the themes Ed Regis uses repeatedly in "The Great Mambo
Chicken..." is that of hubris, defined as:
 
 "scornful, overweening pride; trangression of the divine or moral
  law through ambition, ultimately causing the transgressor's doom."
 
     In many ways, the Extropian philosophy is all about hubris; its
exponents strive to reach heights that are generally considered to be
the preserve of God(s).  One of the top priority items in this
striving is immortality, which seems sensible: living forever
certainly gives one more time to work on the other stuff.
Conversely, practicing (for example) Dynamic Optimism whilst 2 meters
underground in a pine box would be awkward.
 
     For reasons that I will touch on elsewhere, it would be useful
to promote the idea of immortality (via cryonics, for the time being)
in society-at-large, to the point where Fred and Wilma Average think
of death as a long nap in the Whirlpool Frost-Free.  This paper is a
brief look at where we're at in this regard at present, with
particular attention to "the popular culture".  I am using this
phrase as shorthand for "mostly TV and movies", and specifically
exclude from it science fiction in print.
 
                     Cryonics In Popular Culture
 
     Consider cryonics as depicted in popular culture.  One theme
that has been explored on numerous occasions is the "thawing out" of
someone or something that has been placed in "suspended animation".
Without fail, the revived cryonauts are malevolent, ungrateful, and
generally Evil.  In most cases, they do their best to kill their
benefactors and seize control of whatever environment they find
themselves in.  I don't recall any revival scenario where the
until-recently-frozen pop up from their coldsleep capsule and inform
their new friends that Mutual of Omaha will cover any expenses
incurred in the revival process.  Nope, the message is clear: only
bad people get frozen...  either they're fugitives from justice
(often attempting to wait out some statute of limitations) or
prisoners (as in the "Botany Bay" episode of Star Trek) or just bad
people who have grown tired of victimizing their contemporaries
and - "Whoah!  Wouldn't it be fun to victimize some FUTURE people?"
 
     And it gets worse.  Not only are TV/movie cryonauts inevitably
Evil, they are generally rather stupid as well.  They apparently
never even consider such simple measures as leaving revival
instructions where the animate can find them: "The future people will
think we're sissies if we tell them we need an oxygen atmosphere."
They are obviously unconcerned about being revived by a race that
thinks intravenous hydrochloric acid is the breakfast of champions.
And they obviously expect altruistic attitudes will be the norm among
revivers; my own cryonics capsule is going to sport a large "VALUABLE
PRIZE INSIDE" banner, along with easy-to-follow assembly
instructions.  I also plan to follow Egyptian customs, insofar as I
will insist on being frozen with two pieces of ID and my MasterCard.
Movie cryonauts don't seem to worry about that kind of stuff: "Just
make sure you pack my Kill-O-Rama Blaster, doc."
 
     This, though, is pretty blatant stuff.  The simple stories of
movies and TV require conflict, and they're always hard up for
sources thereof.  And hey, maybe we WILL wake up in a future
surrounded by the kind of idiots that populate television scripts,
and want to wipe them out immediately.  If I was revived by the
characters from M*A*S*H, my first impulse would be to blast them all,
and then go back to sleep until a less irritating civilization
evolved.
 
                   Immortality In Popular Culture
 
     A more serious bias can been seen when you look at depictions of
people who want to live forever.  Wishing to avoid going gently into
that good night is clearly the mark of a psychopath, not just in
Hollywood's eyes, but almost universally in human mythology.
Immortality is always associated with evil and corruption; avoiding
death is a Bad Thing.  Two cases in point:
 
     The recent film version of "Dracula" showed the Count being
"inflicted" with immortality as punishment for using inappropriate
language in church.  When, faced with the threat of having Winona
Ryder as a girlfriend for all eternity, he finally smartens up and
apologizes, he is granted "absolution" by being allowed to kick the
bucket.
 
     Of interest to uploading enthusiasts is the sad case of Freddy
Krueger, villain of the "Nightmare on Elm Street" films.  Freddy, for
those of you unfamiliar with the plot, was a serial killer who was
killed by a vigilante group; we're never given the whole story, but I
surmise that Freddy was pursued and captured by a steely-eyed loner
cop who played by his own rules (despite being handcuffed by the
courts), then set free by our revolving-door criminal justice
system.  The occupants of Elm Street (the locale of Freddy's murders)
were annoyed enough by this that they ran Freddy down and tossed him
into a furnace (presumably, they couldn't buy handguns) at the local
school.  Unfortunately for them, Freddy's soul was so Evil that it
stuck around long enough for the children of Elm Street to grow up,
at which point it started terrorizing them by entering their dreams.
Change the terminology a little bit, and you've got an uploaded
persona mucking about in other peoples' virtual realities.  Freddy
was eventually put to rest by burying his bones in holy ground, but
this only lasted from the end of part III to the beginning of part
IV; evidently, there was an offsite backup.
 
     The list goes on...  the "Pet Semetary" films, "Twilight Zone"
episodes from here to eternity, Dorian Gray, vampires, werewolves,
golems...  The concept of Not Dying has become synonymous with "bad";
consider "The House That Wouldn't Die", the tale of a bad house, or
"The Brain That Wouldn't Die", the tale of a bad brain [1].  Anything
that lasts longer than three-score and whatever is suspect.  Even
mere longevity is somehow indicative of evil.  Anything that's been
around for a long time is generally pretty rotten in popular
culture.  From H.P.  Lovecraft on down, we're constantly confronted
with "age-old evil" and "horrors from the dawn of time".  Nowhere do
we find reference to "Evil that was born yesterday" or "Horror from
1977"[2].
 
     The essence of popular immortality, then, can be summarized as
follows:
 
1) Wanting to live forever is Evil.
2) If you're sufficiently Evil, you might live forever.
3) If you live forever, you're Evil.
4) Mind your manners, or you'll be sent to your room without Death.
 
                            Bright Spots
 
     The popular culture picture of the struggle against death is not
all bad, of course.  Witness the popularity of "Cocoon", in which a
group of lovable but neglected senior citizens is scooped up by a
flying saucer to be taken away to a distant planet where they "won't
ever die".  Evidently, for those old enough and sufficiently
obnoxious, immortality is OK, as long as they practice it somewhere
else.  Of more relevance is "Reanimator", in which an obsessive young
medical student, Herbert West, has discovered a serum which can
revive the dead, though not without some unpleasant side effects,
such as marked anti-social tendencies.  Our hero's hubris draws the
wrath of the Dean of Medicine and the med school's resident "grant
machine", who has made his reputation on proving the irreversibility
of death.  In an interesting reversal of the usual cinematic
treatment of overweening pride, West is unscathed and undeterred at
the end of the film, while those who opposed him are effectively
dead. [3]
 
                              So What?
 
     The above discussion raises several important issues that should
be addressed, preferably before Alcor is stormed by an ugly
torch-waving mob of peasants.  The association of immortality with
Evil is going to be a major stumbling block in creating institutions
(read "propagating memes") to sustain and nourish Extropian values.
The ongoing debates in society regarding right-to-life and
right-to-death (among many other things) indicate that there's a lot
of people out there who think that the State owns your body.  That
idea, coupled with an association between immortality and death,
could have some serious consequences for present and future
cryonauts.  It is easy to picture a future where laws arise
forbidding cryonic suspension and/or requiring all those presently in
suspension be thawed out and given a "proper burial".  Once you're
frozen, your only "defense" is the continued existence of a social
structure that'll leave you alone until methods for bringing you back
safely are worked out. [4] It therefore behooves those of us who are
planning to be around for a few more years to start planting some
seeds that may grow into a antidote to the "Evil Immortal" meme.
 
     One approach is to co-opt some of those hoary old Hollywood
themes and put an Extropian twist on them.  One particularly
intriguing idea is that of an "Extropian Beach Party" film, with Max
More in the Frankie Avalon role, Perry Metzger as "Moon Doggie", and
Tim May as the Beaver.  "The happy-go-lucky immortal denizens of
Extropian Beach are challenged to a surfing duel by the local
ephemeral motorcycle gang, 'The Deathists'..."
 
                                 ###
 
 
                                NOTES
 
[1] That brains have an extremely bad reputation in Hollywood is an
understatement, but that topic is an essay in itself.  A friend
of mine [5] was an ardent collector of "motile brain" flicks, and put
it most succinctly: "There's nothing quite so terrifying as a brain
gone bad."
 
[2] Except perhaps in reviews of classic disco music compilations.
 
[3] Though not so dead as to preclude their appearance in a sequel,
"Bride of Reanimator".
 
[4] This could be used as a counter-argument to the ideas of those
whom I term "passive libertarians".  This group is characterized by
the idea that there's nothing one can do to counter the growth of the
state and its associated economic "drag" except wait for it to
collapse, suffer through a few years of abject poverty, and rebuild a
libertarian society on the ruins.  This idea has its merits (given
how futile direct confrontation with Big Brother can be) but ignores
the plight of the suspended, who can't "survive" a collapse of the
economic infrastructure; they need coolant, electricity, and the
annuities that pay for their suspension.
 
[5] This friend is now dead, and therefore presumably a non-Evil and
reliable source of information.
 
